00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.139
There’s a battle going on in the Pacific.

00:00:14.139 --> 00:00:18.880
The People’s Republic of China and the Republic
of China, more commonly known as Taiwan, have

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:24.450
fought their territorial dispute for generations,
but now, nearly each and every one of the

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:29.930
tiny nations dotting the Pacific Ocean has
emerged as a battlefield for this conflict.

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:35.790
The weapons of this war are not guns and bullets,
but rather concrete and banknotes.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:40.580
This is a diplomatic mission aiming to shore
up the support of some of the world’s smallest

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:42.260
countries.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:47.970
There are fourteen nations in question here:
Palau, Papua New Guinea, the Federated States

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:53.170
of Micronesia, the Solomon Islands, Nauru,
Vanuatu, the Marshall Islands, Fiji, Tuvalu,

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:57.010
Tonga, Kiribati, Samoa, Niue, and the Cook
Islands.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:01.739
With the exception of Papua New Guinea, each
of these have populations below one million

00:01:01.739 --> 00:01:06.470
and each also tends to have fairly low GDP
per capita’s—generally between one and

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:08.430
five thousand dollars.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:13.080
This means that they’re each granted fairly
significant amounts of foreign aid.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:20.740
In the previous decade, Niue, for example,
received $134 million in aid, or about $83,000

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.440
for each member of its population.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:28.450
Region-wide that average is only in the low-thousands,
but the point is that the Pacific gets a lot

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:29.450
of aid.

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:33.299
Now, most of this does not come from Taiwan
or China.

00:01:33.299 --> 00:01:36.010
Australia is by far the largest donor.

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:40.909
It is important for Australia to be in these
countries’ good graces for strategic reasons.

00:01:40.909 --> 00:01:45.799
For example, 6% of Australia’s GDP is made
up solely of exports that take one of these

00:01:45.799 --> 00:01:51.119
three maritime routes—each of which passes
by some of these Pacific countries.

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:55.430
These trade routes could be disrupted if there
were instability in the Pacific.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:02:00.060
Political stability also keeps foreign powers
from attempting a power-grab in any of these

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:04.520
nations, and any such power-grab would likely
be too close for comfort for Australia.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:09.269
So, the primary aid motivation for Australia
and its close allies, like New Zealand and

00:02:09.269 --> 00:02:11.939
the US, is stability.

00:02:11.939 --> 00:02:16.739
That is not the primary motivation for China
and Taiwan.

00:02:16.739 --> 00:02:21.489
These two powers are openly engaging in what's
known as "checkbook diplomacy.”

00:02:21.489 --> 00:02:25.249
They're buying diplomatic partners through
financial aid, but why?

00:02:25.249 --> 00:02:29.359
China is a massive nation of more than a billion
people.

00:02:29.359 --> 00:02:34.459
What benefit does it receive from having tiny
nations like Samoa, Tonga, or Fiji on its

00:02:34.459 --> 00:02:35.459
side?

00:02:35.459 --> 00:02:40.629
Well, they might gain some limited benefit,
but the main reason China works to build these

00:02:40.629 --> 00:02:43.989
diplomatic partnerships is so Taiwan can not.

00:02:43.989 --> 00:02:49.340
That’s because, to Taiwan, even a tiny,
eleven-thousand person nation like Tuvalu

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:53.769
is hugely valuable as Tuvalu has something
Taiwan doesn’t.

00:02:53.769 --> 00:02:57.140
Tuvalu is a member of the United Nations.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:01.659
Taiwan is rarely recognized as an independent
country in international settings.

00:03:01.659 --> 00:03:06.800
This is despite the fact that, in almost every
way, it acts and operates as an independent

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:07.800
country would.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:12.120
There are plenty of countries, like the US,
for example, that have strong relations with

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.730
Taiwan and treat it as if it were a country.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:20.290
The US has a de-facto embassy in Taiwan, recognizes
Taiwanese passports, sells military supplies

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:24.829
to Taiwan, and has an enormously strong trade
relationship with Taiwan, yet it does not

00:03:24.829 --> 00:03:26.969
recognize Taiwan as a country.

00:03:26.969 --> 00:03:33.249
That’s because, in international relations,
there is an implicit rule: you either recognize

00:03:33.249 --> 00:03:37.260
the Republic of China or the People’s Republic
of China.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:42.199
Now, given the choice between working with
the world’s most populous country or a moderately-sized

00:03:42.199 --> 00:03:46.510
island of 24 million, nearly every nation
picks China.

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:51.120
Even though Taiwan and the US are functionally
allies while China and the US are frenemies,

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:56.370
at best, it’s just not worthwhile for the
US to recognize Taiwan as a country.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:02.900
This is the case for almost every country—that
is, of course, unless Taiwan makes it worthwhile.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:08.599
Across the Pacific, Taiwan has been funding
projects to keep small nations on its side.

00:04:08.599 --> 00:04:13.530
In Palau, they spent $1.3 million to help
the country recover from Typhoon Haiyan.

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:18.140
In Papua New Guinea, they spent $4.5 million
to improve domestic agriculture in order to

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:19.209
increase food security.

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:24.110
In the Solomon Islands, they spent $2.5 million
to train local staff and conduct a survey

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.100
of the islands’ local plant species.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:31.140
In Nauru, they spent $600 thousand donating
more-efficient compact fluorescent lightbulbs

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:32.720
to local households.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:37.090
In the Marshall Islands, they spent $1 million
building a seawall around Majuro Atoll to

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:39.180
protect it from rising sea-levels.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:44.070
In Fiji, they spent $300 thousand improving
mental health service access around the country.

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:48.840
In Tuvalu, they spent $200 thousand repairing
and improving water storage equipment around

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:50.860
the country after a cyclone.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:56.060
In Kiribati, they spent $19 million to repair
and upgrade the country’s main airport.

00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:00.650
Each of these projects, and the hundreds more
funded by Taiwan around the pacific, improves

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:06.630
its relations with these countries, and therefore
brings it more and more diplomatic clout.

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:11.540
Taiwan can’t afford to buy the support of
large nations, but in some settings the size

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:14.020
of the nation doesn’t matter.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:19.280
In the United Nations General Assembly, for
example, it’s one country, one vote.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.820
It doesn’t matter if it’s Tuvalu or China,
when voting, every member nation has the exact

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:26.300
same power.

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:30.850
There are other systems in the UN where power
is more balanced based on population, but

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:36.720
overall, the world’s smallest nations have
a disproportionate amount of power.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:41.570
More recognition, even by the smallest countries,
strengthens Taiwan’s case for a position

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:44.400
in intergovernmental organizations like the
UN.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:47.680
That’s why Taiwan targets small nations
for support.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:54.040
$5 million spent on Tuvalu goes a lot further
than $5 million spent on Mexico, for example,

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:59.190
but in the General Assembly, Tuvalu can help
Taiwan just as much as Mexico can.

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:04.590
This diplomatic clout that Taiwan has amassed,
though, gets in the way of China’s ambition

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.640
of eventually unifying the two.

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.940
It is for that reason that the People’s
Republic has gone on the offensive.

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:16.910
China has been courting Taiwan’s diplomatic
partners in the Pacific with promises of larger

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:18.020
aid packages.

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:22.830
The most recent two defectors were Kiribati
and the Solomon Islands.

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:28.530
Early in the 2010’s China provided no foreign
aid to either of these countries, but then,

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:34.680
as soon as they each revoked their recognition
of Taiwan, promises of planes, ferries, medical

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:37.900
supplies, and more started pouring in from
China.

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:42.140
In addition to these two countries, Taiwan
has lost diplomatic support from the Dominican

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:48.360
Republic, Burkina Faso, El Salvador, Panama,
and São Tomé and Príncipe since 2016.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:54.940
That leaves them with just fifteen total diplomatic
partners, and only four in the Pacific region.

00:06:54.940 --> 00:07:00.510
To maintain the limited support that it has,
Taiwan has had to get tactical.

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:06.780
China’s style with aid is to build big,
flashy, expensive infrastructure projects.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:12.000
In Fiji, they spent $50 million building a
paved road that halved the time it took for

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.410
local farmers to get to markets, but local
engineers reportedly said that a far lower-quality

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:20.650
and cheaper road could have achieved the same
result.

00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:25.860
In Vanuatu, they spent $80 million upgrading
a port to allow for larger cruise ships to

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:30.670
dock, but in all of 2019, for example, just
five visited.

00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:34.889
In Papua New Guinea, they spent $1 million
building a malaria hospital in the capital

00:07:34.889 --> 00:07:39.410
of Port Moresby, despite that being the region
of the country with the least malaria, and

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:44.080
despite the capital having no road link to
the regions with the worst malaria outbreaks.

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:49.450
Each of these projects and more clearly have
positive effects, but undoubtably they are

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:55.710
not the most efficient uses of $1 or $50 or
$80 million in the mission of improving relations

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:58.650
between China and these Pacific nations.

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:03.810
China does big, flashy, and sometimes ineffective
aid.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:08.340
Taiwan does small, personable, and generally
more effective aid.

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:13.520
That’s why they have been able to maintain
the relationships they have for so long, despite

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:15.669
a much lower budget.

00:08:15.669 --> 00:08:20.590
China works to impress the governments, Taiwan
works to impress the people.

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:25.270
Taiwan’s average aid project size in the
Pacific is $500,000.

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:28.830
China’s is $6 million.

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:32.930
Taiwan does things like contribute $1,600
for Palau’s baseball team to attend a tournament

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:37.880
in Guam, or spend $200,000 buying a boat for
an outer atoll in the Marshall Islands, or

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:41.019
construct $300,000 of solar street lights
in Nauru.

00:08:41.019 --> 00:08:46.690
The $80 million port in Vanuatu might help
people more than the $1,600 for the baseball

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:51.720
team in Palau, but one can be sure that every
member of that team and their families and

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:55.920
friends heard about and remember how Taiwan
helped them.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.589
That’s less likely with the large, somewhat
ineffective port.

00:09:00.589 --> 00:09:05.680
Taiwan knows that the choice of which side
to support is a political issue in the Pacific

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:07.399
because there is that choice.

00:09:07.399 --> 00:09:12.470
Pretty much any politician in these countries
will run their campaign with the Taiwan issue

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:18.079
as part of their platform and so Taiwan is
trying to gain the support of everyday people.

00:09:18.079 --> 00:09:22.839
They want to win support from the ground-up,
rather than from the top-down.

00:09:22.839 --> 00:09:27.569
This might create a better value for their
money, but at the end of the day, China just

00:09:27.569 --> 00:09:30.319
has so much more money.

00:09:30.319 --> 00:09:35.120
If China wants to win the political support
of a small nation, they can just spend and

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:37.350
spend and spend.

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:43.040
At this point, Taiwan’s few, tiny diplomatic
partners are no longer a threat to China’s

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:45.079
goal of reunification.

00:09:45.079 --> 00:09:50.129
China will likely keep courting these nations,
not because they need to, but because it’ll

00:09:50.129 --> 00:09:51.660
further weaken Taiwan.

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:57.029
Eventually, though, if there comes a day when
the Taiwan issue is resolved, either through

00:09:57.029 --> 00:10:02.240
reunification or recognized independence,
the Pacific islands will be victims because,

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:11.839
when it's no longer useful, the pawn is the
first to be sacrificed.

00:10:11.839 --> 00:10:15.339
If you want to learn more about one of the
four Pacific-island nations that are still

00:10:15.339 --> 00:10:20.279
on Taiwan’s side, the Marshall Islands,
you can watch the Nebula-exclusive documentary

